A startup uses the following class, which currently supports debit-card payments. To support Alipay, a developer proposes editing the existing class and adding `processAlipayPayment()`. Explain which design principle this approach conflicts with and suggest an improved design.

```java
class PaymentProcessor {
    void processDebitCardPayment() {
        // Process a debit-card payment.
    }
}
```

answer:
It conflicts with the **Open–Closed Principle (OCP)**: a module should be open to extension and closed to modification. Repeatedly editing the processor for every new payment method makes extensions affect existing code.

Introduce a common interface and supply implementations:

```java
interface PaymentMethod { void processPayment(); }
class DebitCardPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void processPayment() { /* Debit-card processing */ }
}
class AlipayPayment implements PaymentMethod {
    public void processPayment() { /* Alipay processing */ }
}
class PaymentProcessor {
    void process(PaymentMethod paymentMethod) {
        paymentMethod.processPayment();
    }
}
```

A new payment method can be added as another implementation without changing `PaymentProcessor`.

