This Java method should calculate the average of an array. Identify and fix its issues. What should it return for `{10, 20, 30, 40, 50}`?

```java
static double calculateAverage(int[] nums) {
    int sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i <= nums.length; i++) {
        sum += nums[i];
    }
    return (double) sum / nums.length;
}
```

answer:
- Change the loop boundary to `i < nums.length`; `nums[nums.length]` is out of bounds.
- Reject a null or empty array before accessing it or calculating an average.
- Cast before division. The original `(double) sum / nums.length` already does this correctly; it is not an integer-division bug.
- A `long` accumulator also avoids overflow when summing multiple `int` values.

```java
static double calculateAverage(int[] nums) {
    if (nums == null || nums.length == 0)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Array cannot be null or empty");
    long sum = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) sum += nums[i];
    return (double) sum / nums.length;
}
```

For the supplied array, the sum is 150 and the average is **30.0**.

