```

answer:
- Reject null or empty input before reading its first element.
- Initialize `max` to `nums[0]`, not 0; otherwise all-negative arrays can incorrectly return 0, and the first element is not properly included.
- Use `i < nums.length` to avoid an out-of-bounds access.

```java
static int findMaxValue(int[] nums) {
    if (nums == null || nums.length == 0)
        throw new IllegalArgumentException("Array cannot be null or empty");
    int max = nums[0];
    for (int i = 1; i < nums.length; i++) {
        if (nums[i] > max) max = nums[i];
    }
    return max;
}
```

The maximum of the supplied example is **63**. For an all-negative array, the method correctly returns its least-negative element.
